IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- THE SHARED NOUNS of the Food and Nutrition Act of 2008 (United States, 7 U.S.C. ch. 51, the SNAP statute).
-- This module is DECLARE and nothing else: no rule, no #EVAL, no #ASSERT. The six part agents read it and may not edit it.
-- SOURCE: usc07-ch51.txt, a plain-text rendering made by a converter script from the OLRC's USLM XML (release point
-- Public Law 119-121). It is not the official text, and nothing here is "verified against the official text".
-- The cost-of-living figures (fna-snap-cola-fy2027.txt) are INPUT DATA, not statute; only their ROW TYPES live here.
--
-- THE TEST FOR BEING HERE: could a witness of ordinary competence testify to this fact, or does answering it
-- require reading the Act? Only the first kind is a field. "Is an elderly or disabled member", "is a household",
-- "is food" and "is a retail food store" are answers the Act gives, so none of them is a field: a part asks them
-- as an input named for the section that gives them (PLAN.md, SEAMS).
--
-- MONEY is a NUMBER of dollars (not cents). A MONTHLY figure says so in its name. DATES are `Date day month year`
-- (day first); never define a YMD. No name below is a prelude or daydate name (Day, Date, Month, Period are avoided).

-- WHERE a household lives, at the grain the USDA cost-of-living tables use (fna-snap-cola-fy2027.txt, Tables 1 and 2
-- of the allotments, the deductions and the income standards). The Act itself names five places (s 2014(c)(1): the
-- 48 contiguous States and DC, Alaska, Hawaii, the Virgin Islands, Guam). "Rural 1" and "Rural 2" are the USDA's own
-- subdivision of rural Alaska, not the Act's words. A part may coarsen this 7-way split (the income standards lump the
-- 48 States, Guam and the Virgin Islands; the standard deduction lumps Alaska) but cannot refine it.
DECLARE Area IS ONE OF
    `the 48 contiguous States and the District of Columbia`
    `Alaska, urban`
    `Alaska, rural 1`
    `Alaska, rural 2`
    Guam
    Hawaii
    `the Virgin Islands of the United States`

-- WHAT KIND OF PLACE something is, for the definition of "State" in s 2012(r). This is a fact about a place, and it is
-- NOT Area: "State" also takes in the reservations of an Indian tribe, and other places (Puerto Rico, American Samoa)
-- are outside it.
DECLARE `Kind of place` IS ONE OF
    `one of the fifty States`
    `the District of Columbia as a place`
    `the territory of Guam`
    `the territory of the Virgin Islands`
    `a reservation of an Indian tribe`
    `any other place`

-- The immigration facts s 2015(f), s 2014(i) and s 2020(e)(2)(B)(v) all turn on (the list is s 2015(f)(2)(A)-(D)).
DECLARE `Immigration status` IS ONE OF
    `a citizen or national of the United States`
    `an alien lawfully admitted for permanent residence`
    `a Cuban or Haitian entrant`
    `a resident under a Compact of Free Association`
    `any other status`

-- THE PERSON: one individual, as a witness could describe them. Whether the person is an "elderly or disabled
-- member" (s 2012(j)), a "household member", or "eligible" is NOT a field. Facts that only one provision reads
-- (a veteran's disability rating, a conviction, a lottery win) are declared by the part that reads them.
DECLARE Individual HAS
    `age in years`                                                       IS A NUMBER
    `physically and mentally fit`                                        IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 2015(d)(1), (e)(2): an open-textured judgment, taken as a plain fact
    `pregnant`                                                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `immigration status`                                                 IS AN `Immigration status`
    `resident of the United States`                                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `receives supplemental security income benefits`                     IS A BOOLEAN  -- title XVI of the Social Security Act
    `hours worked per week, averaged monthly`                            IS A NUMBER
    `enrolled at least half-time in an institution of higher education`  IS A BOOLEAN

-- ONE ITEM OF INCOME received by a household, per month. Which exclusion of s 2014(d) applies is a rule, so it is not here.
DECLARE `Income item` HAS
    `description`             IS A STRING
    `monthly amount in dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `earned income`           IS A BOOLEAN

-- THE HOUSEHOLD as a case: who is in it, where it lives, what comes in. Its shelter and medical costs are declared by
-- the part that reads them (s 2014(e)), because only that part knows which costs the Act counts.
DECLARE Household HAS
    `the members`      IS A LIST OF Individual
    `where it lives`   IS AN Area
    `the income items` IS A LIST OF `Income item`

-- THE PARTIES the Act puts rights, duties, powers and liabilities on. Shared constructors live only here.
-- The decision-makers are the Secretary, the State agency, a tribal organization, a court and an administrative agency.
-- A seller is a `a retail food store` or `a wholesale food concern` of this type (s 2012(o), s 2018, s 2021).
DECLARE Party IS ONE OF
    `the household`
    `a household member`
    `an applicant household`
    `the State agency`
    `the Secretary`
    `a tribal organization`
    `a benefit issuer`
    `a retail food store`
    `a wholesale food concern`
    `a sponsor of an alien`
    `a court`
    `an administrative agency`

-- THE KINDS OF DECISION a rule would otherwise invent twice (an outcome the Act has a decision-maker reach).
DECLARE `Kind of decision` IS ONE OF
    `certify a household as eligible`
    `deny an application`
    `terminate a certification`
    `reduce an allotment`
    `disqualify an individual`
    `approve a retail food store or wholesale food concern`
    `disqualify a retail food store or wholesale food concern`
    `establish a claim for an overissuance`
    `grant a waiver`

-- HOW LONG a disqualification or an ineligibility lasts (s 2015(b), (d), (h), (j), (p), (q); s 2021(b)).
-- The last two constructors are the "not covered" values: the text hands the length to someone else, and a part
-- must answer with one of them, never with a guessed number.
DECLARE `Period of ineligibility` IS ONE OF
    `for a number of days`   HAS days   IS A NUMBER
    `for a number of months` HAS months IS A NUMBER
    `for a number of years`  HAS years  IS A NUMBER
    `permanently`
    `until a stated condition ends` HAS `the condition` IS A STRING
    `for a period the Secretary prescribes by regulation, not stated in this text`
    `for a period the State agency determines, not stated in this text`

-- WHAT THE GOVERNMENT PAYS: one benefit (s 2012(d)), in the month it is authorized for.
DECLARE `Form of benefit` IS ONE OF
    `an electronic benefit transfer under section 2016(h)`
    `other means of providing assistance, as determined by the Secretary`

DECLARE Benefit HAS
    `value in dollars`      IS A NUMBER
    `form`                  IS A `Form of benefit`
    `month it is for begins` IS A DATE

-- THE CERTIFICATION PERIOD of a household: the period for which it is eligible to receive benefits (s 2012(f)).
DECLARE `Certification period` HAS
    `first day`         IS A DATE
    `length in months`  IS A NUMBER

-- A CLAIM for an overissuance: the amount, who owes it and to whom (s 2022).
DECLARE Claim HAS
    `amount in dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `owed by`           IS A Party
    `owed to`           IS A Party

-- ROW TYPES for the annual cost-of-living tables. The core carries NO FIGURES: the part that encodes the section a
-- table feeds (PLAN.md says which part owns which table) fills a LIST of rows and states the fiscal year.
-- A row is one household size. The USDA's maximum-allotment table has a "9-17" row that is a dollar amount PER ADDITIONAL PERSON
-- above 8, and an "18+" row that is a total: the part that fills that table writes out the rows it means and says which is which
-- (PLAN.md, difficulties). Do not read a row's size as a head-count unless the part's comment says it is one.
DECLARE `Dollars by area` HAS
    `in the 48 contiguous States and the District of Columbia` IS A NUMBER
    `in Alaska, urban`                                          IS A NUMBER
    `in Alaska, rural 1`                                        IS A NUMBER
    `in Alaska, rural 2`                                        IS A NUMBER
    `in Guam`                                                   IS A NUMBER
    `in Hawaii`                                                 IS A NUMBER
    `in the Virgin Islands of the United States`                IS A NUMBER

DECLARE `Table row` HAS
    `household size`  IS A NUMBER
    `the dollars`     IS A `Dollars by area`

DECLARE `Annual table` HAS
    `fiscal year`    IS A NUMBER  -- 2027 is 1 October 2026 to 30 September 2027
    `what it states` IS A STRING
    `the rows`       IS A LIST OF `Table row`
